'''''Salvia leucantha''''', or '''Mexican bush sage''', is a [[herbaceous plant|herbaceous]] [[perennial plant]] in the family [[Lamiaceae]] that is native to subtropical and tropical conifer forests in central and eastern [[Mexico]]. The flowers are usually white, emerging from coloured bracts. It is not frost hardy, but is often grown in warmer latitudes for its prominent arching velvety blue or purple [[inflorescence]]s.<ref name="Clebsch">{{cite book|last1=Clebsch|first1=Betsy|last2=Barner|first2=Carol D.|title=The New Book of Salvias|publisher=Timber Press|year=2003|page=171|isbn=978-0-88192-560-9|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=NM0iwB8GrQYC&pg=PA171}}</ref>

It grows up to {{convert|1.3|m|ft|1|abbr=on}} high and {{convert|2|m|ft|1|abbr=on}} wide, with numerous erect stems, often arching at their tips, and with long inflorescences. The linear-lanceolate leaves are a soft mid-green, with whitish, hairy undersides.<ref name="Clebsch"/>

==Cultivation==
This plant has ornamental value in the garden, and has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/16345/Salvia-leucantha/Details|title=RHS Plant Selector - ''Salvia leucantha''|access-date = 5 March 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | title = AGM Plants - Ornamental | date = July 2017 | page = 94 | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 12 October 2018}}</ref> It is a low-maintenance plant that grows best in full sun and moist but well-drained soils. Flowers attract butterflies, bees and hummingbirds.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?taxonid=281422&isprofile=0&|title=Salvia leucantha - Plant Finder|website=www.missouribotanicalgarden.org|language=en-US|access-date=2018-07-03}}</ref>

==Etymology==
The Latin [[specific epithet]] ''Leucantha'' means 'white-flowered'.<ref name="gledhill">Gledhill, David (2008). ''The Names of Plants''. Cambridge University Press. {{ISBN|9780521866453}} (hardback), {{ISBN|9780521685535}} (paperback). pp 235, 339</ref>

==Uses==
A recent analysis of the essential oil of ''S. leucantha'' found it to be rich in the [[sesquiterpene]] hydrocarbons; β-[[caryophyllene]], α-[[guaiene]], ''cis''-muurola-3,5-diene, [[germacrene]] D, and [[bicyclogermacrene]].{{clarify|It contains these compounds, but no explanation of actual uses (uses for the compounds maybe?)|date=November 2025}} Bornyl acetate constituted 23.9% of the oil.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Negi|first=Anuradha|author2=Javed, Mohammad S|author3=Melkani, Anand B|author4=Dev, Vasu|author5=Beauchamp, Philip S|date=Sep–Oct 2007|title=Steam Volatile Terpenoids from Salvia leucantha|url=http://www.jeoronline.com/archive/detail/30#793|journal=Journal of Essential Oil Research|volume=19|issue=5|pages=463–465|doi=10.1080/10412905.2007.9699953|s2cid=96218518|issn=1041-2905|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

